  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1153 PM MDT Sat Sep 19 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThe vertically stacked low pressure system will continue to slide \neastwards throughout the day. With it will also pull the \nprecipitation over Central MT. However, expect light rain to \ncontinue over Central MT through at least the morning hours. \nIsolated showers may linger in eastern Fergus County through \nSunday afternoon. As this storm system exits, high pressure moves \ninto the region, creating low level stability. The calm \nconditions with breaking in the clouds overnight tonight will \nbring the potential for fog to develop mainly in Southwest MT this\nmorning. Similar conditions set up Monday morning, with the \npotential for fog development to shift into North-Central MT as \nwell.\n\nA drier weather pattern sets up for most of the week as the current \nstorm system moves east and another shortwave slides south of \nMontana across the Great Basin. As this shortwave slides across the \nGreat Basin region, areas near the MT/ID border might get clipped \nwith a few rain showers late Tuesday night. For the second half of \nthe week, southwest flow dominates as the next incoming trough \nbuilds off of the western U.S. coast. Above normal temperatures \nare expected through Friday.\n\nEnsembles hint at this upper-level trough moving through next \nweekend, bringing back chances for precipitation. This trough \nalso pushes temperatures down closer to normal, and may lower \nsnow levels to the higher mountain peaks. -Wilson\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThere still remains some uncertainty in fog development across the \nSouthwest MT valleys this morning. The main uncertainty is forecast \nsoundings also showing low stratus also developing, which could \nprohibit fog potential. The best chances for fog development will be \nin the higher Southwest MT valleys. There's a 10-20% chance for fog \ndevelopment this morning. Monday morning, hi-res guidance shows \ngreater probabilities, with a 30-60% chance for fog with less than a \nmile of visibility along US Highway 2 from Havre to Cut Bank.\n-Wilson",
